\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15.0.1}{\ignorespaces The running tree $T$ with the CF~1187E \emph  {rerooting} overlay. Below each node is the pair $(\mathit  {down}[v],\mathit  {up}[v])$; the answer is their sum $F(v)$. Sanity: at the reference root $\mathit  {down}[1]=\size  (1)=10$, $\mathit  {up}[1]=0$, so $F(1)=10+\dots  $ reads the full $F(1)=30$ once summed over the fold (the pair is the per-node decomposition, not a running total). The maximum is $F(10)=1+47=48$: rooting at the leaf $10$ maximises the sum of subtree sizes. Re-derive every $\mathit  {up}$ value in \S  \ref {sec:c20-twodfs} from $\mathit  {up}[c]=\mathit  {up}[v]+(n-\size  (c))$; the whole table is reproduced as Example~\ref {ex:c20-twodfs-run}.}}{903}{figure.caption.153}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c20-runtree-pair}{{15.0.1}{903}{The running tree $T$ with the CF~1187E \emph {rerooting} overlay. Below each node is the pair $(\mathit {down}[v],\mathit {up}[v])$; the answer is their sum $F(v)$. Sanity: at the reference root $\mathit {down}[1]=\size (1)=10$, $\mathit {up}[1]=0$, so $F(1)=10+\dots $ reads the full $F(1)=30$ once summed over the fold (the pair is the per-node decomposition, not a running total). The maximum is $F(10)=1+47=48$: rooting at the leaf $10$ maximises the sum of subtree sizes. Re-derive every $\mathit {up}$ value in \secref {c20-twodfs} from $\mathit {up}[c]=\mathit {up}[v]+(n-\size (c))$; the whole table is reproduced as \exref {c20-twodfs-run}}{figure.caption.153}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15.0.2}{\ignorespaces The rerooting taxonomy -- the state splits into a down pass and an up pass, joined by the edge-transfer rule. Rows 1--3 are the invertible or trivially-combinable regime; row~4 is the non-invertible regime that needs a prefix/suffix (or exclusive-segment-tree) precompute; row~5 reroots a game value, tying this chapter back to Chapter~14's Grundy theory. Chapter~13's cookbook is the "read at the root only, no up pass" special case of row~1.}}{904}{figure.caption.154}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c20-taxonomy}{{15.0.2}{904}{The rerooting taxonomy -- the state splits into a down pass and an up pass, joined by the edge-transfer rule. Rows 1--3 are the invertible or trivially-combinable regime; row~4 is the non-invertible regime that needs a prefix/suffix (or exclusive-segment-tree) precompute; row~5 reroots a game value, tying this chapter back to Chapter~14's Grundy theory. Chapter~13's cookbook is the "read at the root only, no up pass" special case of row~1}{figure.caption.154}{}}
